---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Landscape from '@/components/tools/calc2/Landscape.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="gravel-calculator">
  <Landscape client:load variant="gravel" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the gravel calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The gravel calculator multiplies the area of the bed by its depth to get the volume, then
      multiplies the volume by the density of the material to get the weight. Suppliers sell gravel by
      the cubic yard, the cubic metre or the ton, so it gives all three.
    </p>
    <pre><code>area          = length × width          (or π × (diameter ÷ 2)² for a circle)
volume (ft³)  = area (ft²) × depth (in) ÷ 12
volume (yd³)  = volume (ft³) ÷ 27
weight (kg)   = volume (m³) × density (kg/m³)
US tons       = weight (kg) ÷ 907.185</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A path of 10 × 10 feet with 3 inches of gravel is 100 ft² × 0.25 ft = 25 ft³, which is
      25 ÷ 27 = 0.926 cubic yards (0.708 m³). At 1,680 kg/m³ that is 1,189 kg, or 1.31 US tons. With 10 %
      extra for settling, order 1.02 cubic yards.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical densities</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Material</th><th>kg/m³</th><th>US tons per yd³</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>Gravel, pea or crushed</td><td>1,680</td><td>1.42</td></tr>
        <tr><td>River rock</td><td>1,600</td><td>1.35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Road base, compacted</td><td>2,080</td><td>1.75</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sand, dry</td><td>1,600</td><td>1.35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Topsoil</td><td>1,280</td><td>1.08</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The animation</h2>
    <p>
      The drawing is a cut through the edge of the bed, to scale. Stones fall into it at the speed gravity
      gives them and fill it to the depth you entered. Stones are drawn at their typical size; sand and
      fine material are drawn larger so the grains stay visible.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Densities are typical values for loose, dry material. Wet stone and compacted base weigh more.</li>
      <li>The ground is taken as flat. Measure low spots and dig-outs separately.</li>
      <li>A compacted base shrinks by 10 to 20 % as it is rolled. Add that to the extra allowance.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
